  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "If $a$ and $b$ are positive numbers, prove that $a^3+b^3\\ge a^2b+ab^2$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a^3+b^3-a^2b-ab^2=(a+b)(a-b)^2\\ge0$.\n\nSince $a+b>0$ and $(a-b)^2\\ge0$, the required inequality follows.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a^3+b^3-a^2b-ab^2&=a^2(a-b)-b^2(a-b)\\\\a^3+b^3-a^2b-ab^2&=(a-b)(a^2-b^2)\\\\a^3+b^3-a^2b-ab^2&=(a+b)(a-b)^2\\ge0.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $a,b>0$, we have $a+b>0$, and every real square is non-negative. Hence $a^3+b^3\\ge a^2b+ab^2$, with equality when $a=b$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
